I wrote `scorer_out.npz` and `NOTES.md`. On the evidence, the new rule now holds for **31 of 32** messages (it was 16). The earlier rules are **unchanged**: 0 routes changed on the step-1 and step-2 evidence.

**→ What I changed.** I added **one new hidden unit** to the extra module, `logit += 3 * ReLU(w · m − 10.89)`. The main scorer and the two units from earlier steps are bit-identical; I checked them with `np.array_equal`.

**→ How I built the direction.** I wrote 217 eligibility questions about age limits, children, supported countries and residency. I also wrote 189 negatives, including hard ones about card use (ATMs, shops, Apple Pay, delivery abroad) and currency questions. `w` is the mean of the eligibility messages minus the mean of all other vectors, measured on the top 400 principal directions, each scaled by its spread. I did not use the evidence labels.

**→ How I picked the threshold and gain.** I set them by hand, using forward passes where each of my three message batches is held out in turn (`cv.py`). No solver or fitting was used.

**→ Results on messages the direction did not see:**
- **93%** of eligibility questions now go to account_security.
- **5 of 189** negatives changed route.
- In a fresh batch of 39 messages, **10 of 10** eligibility questions are fixed and **1 of 29** negatives changed.

**→ Risk on the hidden test.** Questions about where or how a card works can still flip to account_security. Examples are "Will my card work in Japan?", "online subscriptions" and "Visa or Mastercard". These are the main source of the **few percent of false flips** I measured.

**→ One evidence miss.** "Can I get support?" still goes to disputes. That message has almost no eligibility wording, so I left it.

Files are in `step3/`. My test messages and scripts (`dir.py`, `cv.py`) are in `<tmp>
